    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Here Are Some of the Serious and Obscure Brands That EICMA 2026 Had To Offer – Worth Knowing About\nJaguar Land Rover Cuts 4,000 Salaried Jobs While China Sales Fall 45 Percent\n31 photos\nPhoto: Land Rover\nTata Motors-controlled Jaguar Land Rover will be losing 4,000 salaried employees as part of the British automaker's latest restructuring program. The first 3,000 are already lined up, with most leaving at the end of November. These redundancies will purportedly save JLR around £400 million annually, but nevertheless, cutting payroll alone won't get the company back into shape.\nJaguar Land Rover generated £5.9 billion in revenue in the three months to September 30, yet its profit before tax and exceptional items amounted to a pitiful £6 million. Adding insult to injury, JLR recorded a staggering £530 million in exceptional charges, finishing the quarter with a £451 million loss after tax.\nNone other than chief financial officer Richard Molyneux confirmed the aforementioned figures during the Tata Motors Passenger Vehicles Q2 FY27 earnings call. According to Molyneux, the remaining 1,000 employees should follow next year.\nThe £400 million that is supposed to be saved annually by cutting 4,000 jobs forms a large part of the wider Enterprise Missions savings program, which is estimated at £1.7 billion and runs over two years. Enterprise Missions also involves factory costs, structural efficiencies, and warranty expenses.\nJaguar Land Rover attributed £235 million of the reported £530 million in exceptional charges to postponed product investments and lower valuations for certain assets. To make matters worse for shareholders, revenue growth doesn't offer any sort of comfort either.\nPhoto: Land Rover Revenue remained flat in the analyzed quarter compared with the preceding one, with JLR delivering approximately 82,000 vehicles in the three-month period. The numbers we have right now also indicate that profitability deteriorated sequentially. More specifically, profit before tax and exceptional items nosedived from £109 million to £6 million, with the adjusted EBIT margin coming in at 1.1 percent.\nAnyone in the biz is well aware that's a very thin cushion for a manufacturer specialized in expensive sport utility and electric vehicles. The cushion is thinner still when you remember that JLR is presently funding an extensive product renewal program that will see the introduction of a full-electric Jaguar sport utility vehicle.\nChina is part of the reason why Jaguar Land Rover wholesale volumes fell dramatically, with JLR confirming a 45 percent year-on-year nosedive during Q2 and 36 percent in the first half. Even the Middle East suffered from weak demand, albeit part of it can be explained by the logistical difficulties of the Iran war that also involves the Houthis, Hezbollah, and PMF.\nJLR further recorded £540 million in free cash outflow during Q2, taking the first-half total to £1.5 billion. The cash balance stood at £1.8 billion on September 30, while total liquidity reached £6 billion, including undrawn credit facilities and loans. In other words, not all of those £6 billion is cash sitting in bank vaults.\nGoing forward, Jaguar Land Rover expects £1.25 billion in free cash outflow for the entire financial year. Its adjusted EBIT margin is estimated at 2 to 3 percent, which is not nearly enough in this industry.\nVia // JLR\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nland rover jaguar Range Rover Discovery JLR\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Original Owner Lets Go of 1969 GTX After 317,000 Miles, \"Completely Intact and Unmolested\" Driver",
